  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1142 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe primary weather concern remains the intense heat. Jonesboro\nand NWS Memphis recorded high temperatures of 101 degrees on\nWednesday, while the Memphis airport reached a high of 100. These\ntemperatures are not out of the question across much of the\nMidsouth over the next several days, given little change in the\nweather pattern.\n\nWednesday's 19Z NBM, upon which the evening forecast is based,\ncontinued the recent trend of being 3-5 degrees too high on\ndewpoints, and perhaps a degree too cool on daytime temperatures.\nThe majority of the Midsouth is below the 10th percentile soil\nmoisture. Much of eastern Arkansas, and areas of West Tennessee\nto the north and east of Memphis, have soil moisture below the\n2nd percentile. This may be impacting the NBM dewpoints. The\nofficial forecast will blend two parts CONSShort guidance with\none part NBM for dewpoints through Friday. The net result leaves\nus at or above Heat Advisory criteria, but below Extreme Heat\nWarning criteria for most locations. Similar to Wednesday,\nisolated showers and thunderstorms may occur over northwest MS,\nand may provide localized temporary relief from the heat.\n\nFor the longer term, both GEFS and ECMWF ensemble mean 500mb\nheight progs depict the upper level ridge center retrograding\nfrom the Midsouth to the southern plains by this weekend. This\nwill bring light north/northeast flow aloft to the Midsouth, but\nhave only modest effect on surface temperatures. Looking toward\nthe middle of next week, both ensemble model systems show the\nupper ridge expanding back to the east, extending from the\nsouthern plains through the Midsouth and the Southeast. This\npattern continues for at least another week, past September 16th.\nWe may be in for one of our hottest Septembers, barring a\nsignificant pattern shift late in the month.",
